<section id="tips">
<h2>Design Tips for Higher Click-Through</h2>

<p>Good design does not mean complex design. The most effective affiliate visuals are simple, consistent, and benefit-focused. Here are the rules that matter:</p>

<p><strong>Use your brand colours consistently.</strong> Every visual — comparison graphic, pin, CTA button, social share image — should feel like it came from the same source. Canva's Brand Kit makes this automatic if you set it up once.</p>

<p><strong>One visual, one message.</strong> Do not try to communicate three benefits, a price, a rating, and a CTA in one graphic. Each visual should communicate one thing. The comparison graphic communicates the key differences. The CTA button communicates the next action. The pin communicates the article's value proposition. Separate them.</p>

<p><strong>Show the benefit, not the product.</strong> A screenshot of a dashboard is forgettable. An image of what the dashboard enables — more leads, saved time, higher commissions — is memorable. When using AI image generation, describe the outcome, not the tool.</p>

<p><strong>Use contrast for CTAs.</strong> Your CTA colour should appear nowhere else on the page. If your site uses blue text and white backgrounds, make the CTA button orange or green. The eye goes to the element that breaks the pattern. Canva makes it trivial to test different accent colours — create three identical buttons with different fills and A/B test them across articles.</p>

<p><strong>Resize for the platform.</strong> The optimal image dimensions differ: blog headers (1200×628px), Pinterest pins (1000×1500px), Instagram posts (1080×1080px), YouTube thumbnails (1280×720px), and Twitter cards (1200×675px). Canva Pro's Magic Resize adapts a single design to all of them in one click. If you promote content across multiple channels, this feature alone justifies the Pro subscription.</p>
</section>

