<section id="faq">
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<FaqItem question="Can I use Canva's free plan for affiliate marketing?">
<p>Yes. The free plan includes enough AI image generation (50 uses/month) and Magic Write (25 uses/month) to produce visuals for 5–10 articles per month. The main limitation is Background Remover (Pro-only) and the lack of Magic Resize. If you are just starting out, the free plan is sufficient. Upgrade to Pro when you hit the usage limits or need premium features consistently.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="Can I use AI-generated images commercially in affiliate content?">
<p>Yes. Canva's terms allow commercial use of AI-generated images created within Canva, including use in affiliate marketing content, blog posts, social media, and ads. The generated images are royalty-free. However, you cannot claim copyright over AI-generated images — and neither can Canva. This is standard across AI image generators as of 2026.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="What types of visuals convert best for affiliate content?">
<p>Comparison graphics (side-by-side product specs with a clear recommendation) consistently produce the highest click-through rates in affiliate reviews. Pinterest pins targeting informational keywords drive the most long-tail traffic. Custom CTA buttons with benefit-driven copy outperform text links by 30–50% in split tests. Product lifestyle images — showing the product in use, not just a screenshot — build more trust than generic product photos.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="How does Canva's affiliate program work?">
<p>Canva pays a 30% recurring commission on Pro subscriptions referred through your affiliate link. This means you earn commission every month the referred user stays on Pro — not just a one-time payout. The program is managed through Impact Radius. To qualify, you need an active website or content channel with affiliate marketing content. For setup details and requirements, see our <a href="/reviews/canva-ai-review/">Canva AI review</a>.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="Should I create images before or after writing the article?">
<p>After. Write the article first — the structure, headings, and key points determine what visuals you need. Creating images first often results in visuals that do not align with the final content. The exception is the CTA button design, which can be templated once and reused across articles with minor text changes.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="How many images should an affiliate article have?">
<p>At minimum: one header image (1200×628px) for social sharing, one product image per product reviewed, and one comparison graphic if comparing 2+ products. Articles with 5–7 relevant, custom images consistently outperform articles with 1–2 stock images in time-on-page and social shares. Each image should add information — not just decoration.</p>
</FaqItem>
